---
/**
 * Pricing, EN. Twin of `src/pages/prix.astro`.
 *
 * Data-driven for the same reason: it READS `pricing.json` and prints a range
 * only where a primary source exists. Today none does, and the page says so.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pathFor } from '@lib/routes.ts';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '@lib/schema.ts';
import { pricing, services as serviceData } from '@lib/data.ts';
import { SERVICE_SLUGS } from '@data/slugs.ts';
import type { ServiceKey } from '@data/slugs.ts';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const ref = { type: 'static', page: 'pricing' } as const;

const keys = Object.keys(SERVICE_SLUGS) as ServiceKey[];
const rows = keys
  .map((k) => ({ key: k, name: serviceData[locale][k].name, price: pricing[k] }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name, 'en'));
const published = rows.filter((r) => r.price.range !== null);
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  ref={ref}
  title="Foundation repair cost in the Quebec City region"
  description="Why this site publishes no price range for the Quebec City region, and what a quote must spell out before two offers can honestly be compared."
  schema={[
    breadcrumbSchema(locale, [
      { name: "Home", ref: { type: 'home' } },
      { name: "Pricing", ref },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <section class="contenant bloc-page">
    <div class="mesure">
      <h1>What foundation repair costs in the Quebec City region</h1>

      <p class="chapeau">
        The honest answer: <strong>nobody publishes a reliable range for this territory</strong>,
        and this site will not invent one. Here is why, and what serves you better than a number.
      </p>

      <h2>Why no price is shown here</h2>
      <p>
        The figures circulating for foundation repair in Quebec come almost entirely from lead
        generation sites and contractor blogs. These are not primary sources: none of them
        explains the sample, the year or the territory. The most frequently copied figure — a
        linear-foot price for drain replacement — also targets Greater Montreal, not the Quebec
        City Metropolitan Community.
      </p>
      <p>
        Copying those numbers would make this page look informed while sending you into a quote
        with a false expectation. The site's rule is simple: a figure without a verifiable
        public source is worth <strong>nothing</strong>, and the page says so.
      </p>

      {
        published.length === 0 ? (
          <p>
            <strong>As of writing, not one of the site's eleven services has a range published
            by a primary source.</strong> The research is recorded and dated in the site's data
            so it is not repeated identically; the moment a source opens up, it will appear here.
          </p>
        ) : (
          <>
            <h2>The ranges that are actually sourced</h2>
            <ul>
              {published.map((r) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={pathFor({ type: 'service', service: r.key }, locale)}>{r.name}</a>
                  {" — $"}{r.price.range![0]} to ${r.price.range![1]}
                  {r.price.unit ? ` ${r.price.unit}` : ''}
                  {r.price.sourceUrl && (
                    <>
                      {" ("}
                      <a href={r.price.sourceUrl} rel="nofollow noopener">
                        {r.price.sourceLabel ?? 'source'}
                      </a>
                      {`, checked ${r.price.checkedOn})`}
                    </>
                  )}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        )
      }

      <h2>What actually moves the invoice</h2>
      <p>
        A foundation quote turns on access and on the real condition of the structure, not on a
        rate per foot. Two neighbouring houses can be a factor of three apart.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Machine access.</strong> An open lot and a narrow one wedged between two
          neighbours do not call for the same excavation, or the same reinstatement.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The depth of the footing</strong> and the height of wall to be exposed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What has to come out and go back</strong>: deck, steps, asphalt, landscaping, a
          mature hedge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The condition found once the wall is exposed</strong>, which is only known at
          that moment. That is the main reason a firm price quoted over the phone, with no
          inspection, should make you wary.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The presence of iron ochre</strong>, which changes both the work and its
          maintenance.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What to demand before comparing two quotes</h2>
      <p>
        This is where you regain the leverage an approximate figure would never give you. A
        comparable quote has to name, in writing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>the RBQ licence number</strong> and the subcategories it covers — a foundation
          job often touches three, and being licensed to dig does not authorise waterproofing;
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>the standard used and which part of it</strong> — "a BNQ drain", with no number
          and no part, means nothing;
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>the type of pipe</strong> installed and its conformity;
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>what site reinstatement includes</strong>, and what it does not;
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>what would trigger an extra charge</strong>, and on what basis it would be
          calculated.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two quotes that answer those five points can genuinely be compared. Two bare totals
        cannot. The <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'faq' }, locale)}>frequently asked
        questions</a> cover checking a licence in the register.
      </p>

      <h2>Start with the diagnosis</h2>
      <p>
        On almost every problem described here, the largest cost gap is not between two
        contractors: it is between <em>diagnosing</em> and <em>excavating</em>. A
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'service', service: 'inspection' }, locale)}>drain camera
        inspection</a> tells you whether the drain is clogged or crushed, and that distinction
        separates a cleaning from a full excavation.
      </p>

      <p class="cta-fin">
        <a href={pathFor({ type: 'static', page: 'quote' }, locale)}>Request a quote</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
